Task name: /root
Sender: /root/grammar_kernel_design
Payload:
The reset is feasible on `@mkbabb/parse-that@1.0.0`; no engine uplift is required for the first values+color vertical. The mirror needs one legitimate lexical layer, but no feature scanner.

## What the “scanner” should become

There is one defensible character-recognition boundary:

- CSS preprocessing;
- atomic CSS tokens and trivia;
- recursive component values composed with parse-that.

There should be no `splitTopLevel`, `splitValueTokens`, `balancedUntil`, whole-remainder `complete(parseString)`, `source.endsWith(")")`, or feature-local delimiter walk. The only custom `Parser` leaves should recognize one atomic token/trivia item and return its span. Comments may use a single `indexOf("*/")` inside that atomic trivia leaf; that is a shared CSS Syntax operation, not a feature parser.

## Exact minimal module lattice

```text
mirror/grammar/css/l4/
  syntax/
    source.ts             SourceText preprocessing + original/processed map
    atom.ts               sole sticky-regex/custom atomic-leaf constructor
    trivia.ts             whitespace/comment atoms; trivia0
    tokens.ts             CSS Syntax token families
    component-value.ts    function/simple-block recursion and component lists
  values/
    value-unit.ts         number/percentage/dimension interpretation
    value.ts              legacy CssScalar/CssCall/CssList overlay
  color/
    channel.ts            numeric/none/angle/alpha channel grammars
    color.ts              named/hex/functional/color() grammar and factories
```

This aligns semantically with BBNF’s `tokens.bbnf`, `value-unit.bbnf`, `values.bbnf`, and `color.bbnf`. BBNF’s `func-body.bbnf` should not be copied: the shared component-value grammar replaces that balanced-body seam. Existing `grammar/value.ts` and `grammar/color.ts` should become thin public adapters during migration, then disappear or re-export the new modules.

The checked-out BBNF grammar is useful as a module-boundary witness, not yet as an uplift or semantic oracle. Its current token regexes are incomplete CSS Syntax and its `color.bbnf` separator formulation can mix comma and space forms. Nothing from it should be claimed as hardened without the required acknowledgement.

## Shared token contract

```ts
type CssTrivia =
    | { kind: "whitespace"; span: Span; raw: string }
    | { kind: "comment"; span: Span; raw: string };

type Lexed<T> = Readonly<{
    value: T;
    span: Span;
    leading: readonly CssTrivia[];
}>;

type CssToken =
    | IdentToken | FunctionToken
    | NumberToken | PercentageToken | DimensionToken
    | StringToken | HashToken
    | DelimToken | PunctuationToken;
```

`atom.ts` owns the only low-level constructor:

```ts
function atomic<T>(
    pattern: RegExp,
    project: (match: RegExpExecArray, start: number, end: number) => T,
): Parser<T> {
    // sticky regex only; on failure consume nothing and do not mutate value
    // on success consume exactly one CSS token
}
```

`tokens.ts` exports raw atomic parsers and trivia-bearing variants:

```ts
const triviaPiece = any(cssWhitespace, cssComment);
const trivia0 = triviaPiece.many();

function lexed<T>(token: Parser<T>): Parser<Lexed<T>> {
    return trivia0.then(token).map(([leading, value]) => ({
        value,
        leading,
        span: value.span,
    }));
}

export const token = {
    ident, function, number, percentage, dimension, string, hash,
    comma, slash, colon, semicolon,
    openParen, closeParen, openBracket, closeBracket, openBrace, closeBrace,
};
```

Trivia belongs before a token; tokens never destructively consume trailing trivia. Consequently:

- `a/**/b` remains two identifier tokens;
- trivia before a closer belongs to the closer;
- top-level trailing trivia is captured separately;
- modern color syntax can require a real token boundary by checking `leading.length > 0`;
- exact interval proof remains possible.

Do not use parse-that’s exported `whitespace`, `.trim()`, or `skipBlockComments`: those discard structure and do not implement the required CSS trivia contract.

## Component-value grammar

The component parser is parameterized by its expected closer. It excludes only that closer; mismatched closing tokens remain ordinary component values, as CSS Syntax requires.

```ts
type Stop = ")" | "]" | "}" | null;

function component(stop: Stop, depth: number): Parser<CssComponentValue> {
    return Parser.lazy(() => any(
        functionComponent(depth),
        block("(", ")", depth),
        block("[", "]", depth),
        block("{", "}", depth),
        preservedTokenExcept(stop),
    ));
}

function children(stop: Exclude<Stop, null>, depth: number) {
    return component(stop, depth).many();
}

function functionComponent(depth: number) {
    return token.function.chain(open =>
        children(")", depth + 1)
            .then(token.closeParen)
            .map(([items, close]) => ({ kind: "function", open, items, close })),
    );
}

function block(openText, closeText, depth) {
    return opening(openText)
        .then(children(closeText, depth + 1))
        .then(closing(closeText))
        .map(([[open, items], close]) => ({ kind: "block", open, items, close }));
}

const componentDocument =
    component(null, 0).many()
        .then(trivia0)
        .skip(eof())
        .map(([items, trailing]) => ({ items, trailing }));
```

Use a depth-indexed lazy parser cache, such as `componentAt(depth, stop)`, with a fixed resource limit. Do not close `Parser.lazy` over mutable per-parse depth: lazy parsers cache their first constructed parser globally.

## Values overlay preserving current shapes

The legacy public AST can be produced directly from shared token parsers:

```ts
const valueAtom = any(
    concreteColor.map(colorScalar),
    genericCall,
    numericScalar,
    quotedScalar,
    operatorScalar,
    identScalar,
);

const spaceGroup = valueAtom.many(1)
    .map(items => collapse("space", items));

const slashGroup = spaceGroup.sepBy(token.slash, 1)
    .map(items => collapse("slash", items));

const commaGroup = slashGroup.sepBy(token.comma, 1)
    .map(items => collapse("comma", items));

const valueRoot = commaGroup
    .then(trivia0)
    .skip(eof())
    .map(([value]) => value);
```

This declaratively preserves the existing precedence: comma outside slash outside token sequence. EOF or the required function closer rejects empty and trailing separators.

Function calls are real recursion, not remainder extraction:

```ts
const nestedValue = Parser.lazy(() => commaGroup);

const genericCall = token.function.chain(head => {
    if (COLOR_NAMES.has(head.value.decodedLower)) return fail("CSS color");

    if (ZERO_ONLY.has(head.value.decodedLower)) {
        return token.closeParen.map(() => call(head, []));
    }

    const body = MAY_BE_EMPTY.has(head.value.decodedLower)
        ? nestedValue.opt()
        : nestedValue;

    return body.skip(token.closeParen).map(value => {
        if (value === undefined) return call(head, []);
        return call(
            head,
            value.kind === "list" && value.separator === "comma"
                ? value.items
                : [value],
        );
    });
});
```

`parseCssScalar`, `parseCssValue`, and `parseCssValues` retain the existing `ParseResult` and `CssScalar | CssCall | CssList` shapes. `parseCssValues` only adds the current singleton space-list wrapper.

## Color grammar

Keep current concrete output families and thirteen result spaces. Do not add `color-mix`, relative-color evaluation, math, or substitution in this vertical.

Channels consume shared numeric/ident tokens and retain leading trivia until spacing checks complete:

```ts
const modernRow =
    channel0
        .then(requireGap(channel1))
        .then(requireGap(channel2))
        .then(token.slash.next(alphaChannel).opt())
        .map(([[[a, b], c], alpha]) => ({
            channels: [a.value, b.value, c.value],
            alpha: alpha?.value ?? 1,
        }));

const legacyRow =
    channel0.skip(token.comma)
        .then(channel1.skip(token.comma))
        .then(channel2)
        .then(token.comma.next(alphaChannel).opt())
        .map(([[[a, b], c], alpha]) => ({
            channels: [a.value, b.value, c.value],
            alpha: alpha?.value ?? 1,
        }));

const rgbBody = any(legacyRow, modernRow);
const hslBody = any(legacyHslRow, modernHslRow);
const hwbBody = modernHwbRow;
// lab/lch/oklab/oklch: modern only
```

`requireGap()` accepts whitespace or comment trivia; it is a semantic guard on the already parsed `Lexed<T>`, not a raw-source search.

Function routing parses the function token once:

```ts
const functionalColor = token.function.chain(head => {
    const row = FUNCTION_ROWS.get(head.value.decodedLower);
    return row === undefined
        ? fail("CSS color")
        : row.skip(token.closeParen).map(applyFactory);
});
```

`color()` is:

```ts
const colorFunctionBody =
    colorSpace
        .then(requireGap(normalizedChannel))
        .then(requireGap(normalizedChannel))
        .then(requireGap(normalizedChannel))
        .then(token.slash.next(alphaChannel).opt())
        .map(...);
```

Named colors are `ident` plus a table lookup; hex is one shared hash token plus exact 3/4/6/8 semantic validation. Context classifications (`currentColor`, system colors, `var()`, `env()`, leading relative-color `from`) must be token/name branches, never `startsWith()` or `containsWord()` raw searches. They remain rejected with `color_context_required`, so this does not broaden semantics.

`parseCssValue` uses the `concreteColor` parser object directly. It must never call `parseCssColor()` on a sliced substring.

## parse-that 1.0.0 traps confirmed from installed implementation

- `any()`/`.or()` restore offset and error state, but not the prior value. Atomic failures must not mutate `state.value`.
- `chain()` has no checkpoint. A failed continuation leaves the first parser consumed unless an enclosing alternative rolls it back.
- `many(min)` and `sepBy(min)` do not restore their initial checkpoint when the minimum is unmet.
- `sepBy()` rolls a trailing separator back and succeeds. A required closer or `.eof()` is what makes `a,` invalid.
- `.parse()` returns `state.value` even on error. A demonstrated `string("a").eof().parse("aX")` returns `"a"`. Public adapters must use `.parseState()` and inspect `isError`.
- Exported `whitespace` is `/\s*/`; `.trim()` uses whitespace only and loses trivia.
- `skipBlockComments()` discards whitespace/comments and stops before an unterminated comment.
- `dispatch()` is ASCII first-code-unit dispatch only. Overlapping keys overwrite earlier table entries. Use it only for proven disjoint buckets after profiling; function-name routing belongs in `functionToken.chain(mapLookup)`.
- A zero-width `regex()` fails at EOF because the implementation rejects before executing the regex.
- `Parser.lazy()` caches its constructed parser. Recursion must consume an opener before descent and must use fixed depth-indexed parsers, not mutable parse-local closures.
- `wrap(discard=false)` routes through `all()`, whose implementation drops `undefined` values; do not use it for positional tuples containing optionals.

## Architectural acceptance gate

The replacement vertical should be rejected unless all are true:

- `new Parser(...)` occurs only in `syntax/atom.ts` and one generic failure/guard helper;
- no feature file reads `state.src`, slices a remainder, walks characters, or searches for a closer;
- no `splitTopLevel`, `splitValueTokens`, `balancedUntil`, `completeBody`, or `complete(parseFn)` remains;
- all public roots require EOF;
- components, calls, lists, modern/legacy color rows, and nesting are visibly expressed by combinators;
- comment-boundary, trailing-separator, mixed-color-separator, deep-nesting-limit, hostile/no-throw, and current public-shape tests are GREEN;
- source/spec/browser behavior, not the old regex parser, adjudicates fixtures that change because comments now have correct token boundaries.

No files were edited.

